{
  "subtype": "causal_attribution",
  "outcome_variable": "state_declined",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "is_travel_related",
      "causal_role": "other",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "Flags travel-related expenses, which the query already scopes to ('travel expenses'). It functions as a scoping/filter indicator rather than a treatment or confounder cause of declines."
    },
    {
      "name": "asset_category",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The type/category of expense item is a candidate cause whose effect on decline could be attributed (some categories may be more likely declined)."
    },
    {
      "name": "expense_entry_origin",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "How the expense entered the system (automated vs manual/explicit) could plausibly influence both the category/handling of the expense and its likelihood of being declined."
    },
    {
      "name": "has_source_id",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Presence of a source reference could affect both entry origin/completeness and the decline decision, potentially a common cause; but it is closer to a data-completeness proxy and its role is weakly justified."
    },
    {
      "name": "processing_lag_category",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Processing delay likely lies on the path between how/when an expense is handled and the final decline decision (or is itself downstream of processing), so it is a mediator/outcome-adjacent variable, not a clean common cause."
    },
    {
      "name": "department_group",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Department plausibly causes both the type/pattern of expenses submitted and the decline rate (different policies/scrutiny), a genuine common cause surfaced from data."
    },
    {
      "name": "asset_action_type",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The kind of action (new acquisition, procurement, etc.) is another candidate cause whose effect on declines could be attributed."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["asset_category", "asset_action_type"],
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "expense_entry_origin", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.55},
    {"name": "department_group", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.7},
    {"name": "has_source_id", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.4}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.45,
  "rationale": "The added columns do supply candidate treatments (asset_category, asset_action_type) and several confounders surfaced from the data rather than the query (department_group is the strongest genuine common cause; expense_entry_origin and has_source_id are plausible but weaker). However, the set is muddied: is_travel_related merely restates the query scope, processing_lag_category is a mediator/outcome-adjacent variable that would bias attribution if adjusted for, and much of the schema appears oriented to asset/procurement rather than a clean treatment-vs-confounder separation around declines. The causal-estimation structure is partially realised but confounder quality is moderate at best."
}

